{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.md","line":344,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex#def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold]]","[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","label":"def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","line":630,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Coordinate representation of a metric]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0033}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold, conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation}\n\n  In coordinates $x=(x^1,\\ldots,x^n)$, a Riemannian metric is\n  \\[\n    g=g_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j,\n    \\qquad g_{ij}=g(\\partial_i,\\partial_j).\n  \\]\n  The smooth matrix $(g_{ij})$ is symmetric and positive definite. This identity\n  follows by writing $v=dx^i(v)\\partial_i$ and\n  $w=dx^j(w)\\partial_j$.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Coordinate representation of a metric] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368312d636f6f7264696e6174652d6d6574726963\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0033</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_RiemannianMetrics%23def%3Apet-ch1-riemannian-manifold\">↗ def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_RiemannianMetrics%23conv%3Apet-ch1-einstein-summation\">↗ conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation</a> <p></p>In coordinates <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$x=(x^1,\\ldots ,x^n)$\">$x=(x^1,\\ldots ,x^n)$</span>, a Riemannian metric is <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"true\" data-tex=\"\\[  g=g_{ij}\\, dx^i dx^j, \\qquad g_{ij}=g(\\partial _i,\\partial _j).  \\]\">\\[  g=g_{ij}\\, dx^i dx^j, \\qquad g_{ij}=g(\\partial _i,\\partial _j).  \\]</span> The smooth matrix <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$(g_{ij})$\">$(g_{ij})$</span> is symmetric and positive definite. This identity follows by writing <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$v=dx^i(v)\\partial _i$\">$v=dx^i(v)\\partial _i$</span> and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$w=dx^j(w)\\partial _j$\">$w=dx^j(w)\\partial _j$</span>. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-coords","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","label":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation","title":"conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation","label":"conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-coords","title":"ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-coords","label":"ex:pet-ch1-euclidean-coords"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#ex:pet-ch1-polar-coords","title":"ex:pet-ch1-polar-coords","label":"ex:pet-ch1-polar-coords"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#rem:pet-ch1-ex-6","title":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-6","label":"rem:pet-ch1-ex-6"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","title":"def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric","tex":"\\begin{definition}[Coordinate representation of a metric]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch1-coordinate-metric}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0033}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold, conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation}\n\n  In coordinates $x=(x^1,\\ldots,x^n)$, a Riemannian metric is\n  \\[\n    g=g_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j,\n    \\qquad g_{ij}=g(\\partial_i,\\partial_j).\n  \\]\n  The smooth matrix $(g_{ij})$ is symmetric and positive definite. This identity\n  follows by writing $v=dx^i(v)\\partial_i$ and\n  $w=dx^j(w)\\partial_j$.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","title":"def:pet-ch1-riemannian-manifold","description":""},{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_RiemannianMetrics#conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation","title":"conv:pet-ch1-einstein-summation","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}